<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daria Daulat Bagh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daria Daulat Bagh is the summer palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, built in 1784 in the Indo-Islamic style and set in a large garden. Its teak walls are covered with painted murals, among them a large scene of the victory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and Tipu over the British at Pollilur in 1780. The palace now houses a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daria-daulat-bagh.jpg" alt="Daria Daulat Bagh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rahulpurushot · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gumbaj_containing_tomb_of_Tipu_Sultan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name means Garden of the Sea of Wealth. Srirangapatna is an island in the river Kaveri, about 14 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and the sultan, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tiger of Mysore</a>, ruled the kingdom from this palace for a short time. The building has a rectangular plan on a raised platform, with open corridors along all four sides and wooden pillars at the edge of the plinth. The western and eastern wings have walls, while the other two have recessed bays with pillars holding up the roof. Four partition walls divide the audience hall into four corner rooms, with a central hall joining the eastern and western corridors, and the four staircases are hidden inside those walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Every surface of walls, pillars, canopies and arches carries colourful frescoes in the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> painting. The outer walls show battle scenes and portraits, the inner walls scrolls of thin foliage and flowers, and the wooden ceilings are covered with painted canvas.</p><p>The western wall shows the Battle of Pollilur near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> in 1780: the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and his son marching to battle, Colonel Bailee troubled in his palanquin as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> army surrounds the English, French soldiers under Colonel Lally with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> arriving too late to help his English allies. The eastern wall shows, in five rows, the courts of the sultan's contemporaries, among them the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a>, the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a>, Balaji Rao II Peshwa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnaraja-wodeyar-ii">Krishnaraja Wodeyar II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Tippu Sultan Museum on the top floor holds memorabilia of the ruler, European paintings and Persian manuscripts. Among them is Storming of Srirangapattanam, an oil painting made in 1800 by Sir Robert Ker Porter, showing the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> on 4 May 1799, with the sultan's men resisting the British army and the palace and the minarets of the mosque behind the fort walls. Nearby stand the ruined fort where the sultan died fighting the British, the ruins of his Lal Mahal palace, and outside the fort the Gumbaz, which holds his tomb and those of his father and mother.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daria Daulat Bagh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's legacy is visible in Karnataka today: the Mysore Dasara festival, the silk industry of the region, the fortifications and <b>Daria Daulat Bagh</b> palace at Srirangapatna, and Lalbagh in Bangalore.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
